    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/1609.01133v2</id>
    <title>On permutation numerical semigroups</title>
    <updated>2016-09-26T15:31:06Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.01133v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1609.01133v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>In this paper we introduce the notion of $n$-permutation numerical semigroup. While there are just three $2$-permutation numerical semigroups, there are infinitely many $n$-permutation numerical semigroups if $n &gt; 2$. We construct $16$ families of $3$-permutation numerical semigroups and one family of $n$-permutation numerical semigroups. Finally we present some experimental data, which seem to support a conjecture about the classification of $3$-permutation numerical semigroups.</summary>
    <category term="math.NT" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2016-09-05T12:54:38Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>45 pages. Introduction revised. Fixed some inaccuracies in the classification of 2-permutation semigroups. Some proofs in Section 3 have been revised and two families have been merged.. Section 4 has been revised including some experimental data</arxiv:comment>
